[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Pagination in Elasticsearch: From/Size, Scroll, and Search After"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Retrieving large amounts of data from Elasticsearch requires choosing the right pagination strategy. Depending on your use case, whether it is deep pagination, real-time data export, or standard UI paging, Elasticsearch offers different mechanisms. This tutorial explores From/Size, the Scroll API, and Search After."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Standard Pagination: From and Size"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'from' and 'size' parameters work similarly to the SQL LIMIT and OFFSET clauses. The 'from' parameter defines the offset from where it should start fetching, and 'size' defines the maximum number of hits to return."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"GET /products/_search
{
  "from": 0,
  "size": 10,
  "query": {
    "match_all": {}
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Simple to implement and ideal for traditional web pagination where users jump across pages."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Suffers from the 'deep pagination' problem. Elasticsearch must coordinate across all shards to fetch 'from + size' documents, causing high CPU and memory consumption. It is heavily restricted by the 'index.max_result_window' setting (default is 10,000)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Deep Pagination with the Scroll API"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need to retrieve millions of records (e.g., for data exports or bulk processing), the Scroll API is designed to handle this by taking a frozen snapshot of the index at the time of the initial search."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"GET /products/_search?scroll=1m
{
  "size": 1000,
  "query": {
    "match_all": {}
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The initial request returns a '_scroll_id'. You then pass this ID to the scroll endpoint to retrieve the next batch of results."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"POST /_search/scroll
{
  "scroll": "1m",
  "scroll_id": "DXF1ZXJ5QW5kRmV0Y2gBAAAAAAAAAD4WZXNfWlJqZF93S0pYV2N2NnlJUEhNdw=="
}"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Highly efficient for retrieving large, non-real-time result sets sequentially."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Keeps context alive on the data nodes, consuming memory. Not suitable for real-time user requests because changes made to the index after the initial search are not reflected."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Modern Deep Pagination: Search After"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The recommended approach for deep pagination in modern Elasticsearch versions is 'search_after'. It uses live values from the previous document to pagination forward, avoiding stateful memory overhead on the cluster."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"GET /products/_search
{
  "size": 10,
  "query": {
    "match_all": {}
  },
  "sort": [
    { "price": "asc" },
    { "_id": "asc" }
  ]
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"From the last hit in the response, you extract the sort values (e.g., [10.99, "prod_123"]) and pass them into the next request using the 'search_after' parameter."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"GET /products/_search
{
  "size": 10,
  "query": {
    "match_all": {}
  },
  "search_after": [10.99, "prod_123"],
  "sort": [
    { "price": "asc" },
    { "_id": "asc" }
  ]
}"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Stateless, scalable, highly performant, and allows deep pagination without exhausting cluster memory."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Does not support random access (jumping directly to page 50). You must traverse sequentially."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary Recommendation"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use From/Size for standard UI pagination with small result sets. Use Scroll for background bulk processing where statefulness is acceptable. Use Search After for infinite scrolling feeds and large-scale sequential data traversal."}]
